ss: Vehicles may exhibit unique asymmetry patterns (captured in skewness). Z-scoring normalizes this to enhance interpretability and resilience against the range and outliers in the skewness values.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 3: Create SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION Interaction with SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS\ndf['RADIATION_MAJOR_VARIANCE'] = df['SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION'] * df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS']\n# Feature description: This feature multiplies the scaled radius of gyration and scaled variance along the major axis, representing a compound measure of spread and deviation in silhouette structure.\n# Usefulness: The combined measure reflects how the distribution of mass along the silhouette's major axis interacts with the overall spread, helping differentiate vehicles with unique structural layouts.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Optional: Dropping redundant or noisy columns that may not add value\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\n# SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS might not add significant value for classification, as it is highly concentrated around small values (e.g., mean = 6.31, Q3 = 9). Its low range and potential skewness to low values may not provide sufficient distinguishing power for the task.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['HOLLOWS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\n# HOLLOWS_RATIO appears to have a very narrow range (181–211) and low variability (std = 7.33). This may indicate limited ability to differentiate between vehicle types.\n```end"]
